DEEP[29]=("<i>Yathā pradīptaṃ jvalanaṃ pataṅgā</i>, “as moths into a blazing fire”. <i>Viśanti nāśāya samṛddha-vegāḥ</i>, “rush to destruction with great speed”. <i>Tathaiva nāśāya viśanti lokās</i>, “so these people rush to destruction”. <i>Tavāpi vaktrāṇi samṛddha-vegāḥ</i>, “into your mouths with great speed”.",
 "Two images in a row: rivers (11.28) and moths. Some commentators, like Madhusūdana, contrast them. The rivers stand for the good, who flow naturally into the Lord; the moths for the wicked, drawn by craving to destruction. A popular Sanskrit verse says the moth dies by sight, the deer by sound, the elephant by touch, the fish by taste and the bee by smell. If one sense can destroy, what of a person ruled by all five?",
 "What attracts us is not always good for us. Watch which flames you keep flying toward.")
DEEP[30]=("<i>Lelihyase grasamānaḥ samantāl</i>, “you lick your lips, devouring on all sides”. <i>Lokān samagrān vadanair jvaladbhiḥ</i>, “all the worlds with flaming mouths”. <i>Tejobhir āpūrya jagat samagraṃ</i>, “filling the whole world with brilliance”. <i>Bhāsas tavogrāḥ pratapanti viṣṇo</i>, “your fierce rays scorch it, Viṣṇu”.",
 "<i>Lelihyase</i>, “you lick repeatedly”, is a startling verb for God. Commentators do not soften it. Śaṅkara says it shows the Lord as destroyer at the end of time. Rāmānuja says it shows the Lord’s power over the warriors who will die. Arjuna again calls him Viṣṇu. The Kaṭha Upaniṣad (1.2.25) says for the Self, priests and warriors are food, and death is the sauce: even death is eaten by the Supreme.",
 "Time consumes all things we build. Only what we truly are is beyond its reach.")
DEEP[31]=("<i>Ākhyāhi me ko bhavān ugra-rūpo</i>, “tell me who you are, so fierce in form”. <i>Namo’stu te deva-vara prasīda</i>, “salutations to you, best of gods; be gracious”. <i>Vijñātum icchāmi bhavantam ādyaṃ</i>, “I wish to know you, the primal one”. <i>Na hi prajānāmi tava pravṛttim</i>, “for I do not understand your purpose”.",
 "<i>Pravṛtti</i>, “purpose” or “activity”: Arjuna asks not only who but why. Commentators note that he combines three things: a question, a salutation and a prayer for grace. This is a model of approaching mystery. The question draws out the most famous answer in the chapter.",
 "When life shows its fierce face, bow, ask what it means, and ask for grace to understand.")
DEEP[32]=("<i>Kālo’smi loka-kṣaya-kṛt pravṛddho</i>, “I am Time, grown mighty, destroyer of worlds”. <i>Lokān samāhartum iha pravṛttaḥ</i>, “engaged here in destroying people”. <i>Ṛte’pi tvāṃ na bhaviṣyanti sarve</i>, “even without you, none will survive”. <i>Ye’vasthitāḥ pratyanīkeṣu yodhāḥ</i>, “of the warriors arrayed in the opposing armies”.",
 "<i>Kāla</i> means both time and death. Kṛṣṇa answers <i>pravṛtti</i> with <i>pravṛttaḥ</i>: “my purpose is this”. Commentators note “even without you”: Arjuna’s refusal would not save anyone, only remove him from his role. Oppenheimer, who had studied Sanskrit, said this verse came to his mind after the Trinity test of July 1945. The verse is also read gently: the old must pass for the new to come.",
 "Many things will happen with or without us. Our choice is the part we play.")
DEEP[33]=("<i>Tasmāt tvam uttiṣṭha yaśo labhasva</i>, “therefore stand up, win glory”. <i>Jitvā śatrūn bhuṅkṣva rājyaṃ samṛddham</i>, “conquer enemies, enjoy a prosperous kingdom”. <i>Mayaivaite nihatāḥ pūrvam eva</i>, “by me they are already slain”. <i>Nimitta-mātraṃ bhava savyasācin</i>, “be merely the instrument, Savyasācin”.",
 "<i>Uttiṣṭha</i>, “stand up”, returns from 2.3, 2.37 and 4.42. Here it comes with a vision, not an argument. <i>Nimitta-mātra</i>, “instrument only”, is a key teaching for action without ego. Śaṅkara says the instrument does not bear the sin. Rāmānuja says Arjuna, as the Lord’s instrument, will share in the glory. Many devotees pray: “make me an instrument”. St Francis’s prayer, “make me an instrument of your peace”, is a well-known parallel.",
 "Do your part as an instrument of the good. Let go of owning the outcome.")
DEEP[34]=("<i>Droṇaṃ ca bhīṣmaṃ ca jayadrathaṃ ca</i>, “Droṇa and Bhīṣma and Jayadratha”. <i>Karṇaṃ tathānyān api yodha-vīrān</i>, “and Karṇa and other heroes”. <i>Mayā hatāṃs tvaṃ jahi mā vyathiṣṭhā</i>, “slain by me, slay them; do not be distressed”. <i>Yudhyasva jetāsi raṇe sapatnān</i>, “fight; you will conquer your rivals”.",
 "Bhīṣma could die only when he chose. Jayadratha’s father had cursed that whoever made his son’s head fall would have his own head burst. Arjuna later shot the head into the father’s lap, so the father let it fall himself. Droṇa laid down his arms after hearing a false report of his son’s death. Karṇa was killed when his chariot wheel sank, by a curse. In each case, the outcome came through events Arjuna did not control. <i>Mā vyathiṣṭhāḥ</i>, “do not be distressed”, prepares for <i>mā śucaḥ</i> (18.66).",
 "The obstacles that look unbeatable may already be weaker than they seem. Do your part and see.")
DEEP[35]=("<i>Etac chrutvā vacanaṃ keśavasya</i>, “hearing these words of Keśava”. <i>Kṛtāñjalir vepamānaḥ kirīṭī</i>, “the crowned one, trembling, folded hands”. <i>Namaskṛtvā bhūya evāha kṛṣṇaṃ</i>, “bowing, spoke again to Kṛṣṇa”. <i>Sa-gadgadaṃ bhīta-bhītaḥ praṇamya</i>, “in a choked voice, terrified, bowing”.",
 "<i>Gadgada</i>, a voice choked with emotion, is a sign of deep devotion in bhakti texts; the Bhāgavata (11.14.24) lists it among the marks of a true devotee. Sañjaya is reporting to Dhṛtarāṣṭra, whose own sons have just been seen in the Lord’s mouths. Some commentators hear in Sañjaya’s pause a last hint to the king: stop the war.",
 "Some news is meant to wake us before it is too late. Listen to it.")
DEEP[36]=("<i>Sthāne hṛṣīkeśa tava prakīrtyā</i>, “rightly, Hṛṣīkeśa, at your praise”. <i>Jagat prahṛṣyaty anurajyate ca</i>, “the world rejoices and delights”. <i>Rakṣāṃsi bhītāni diśo dravanti</i>, “demons flee in fear in all directions”. <i>Sarve namasyanti ca siddha-saṅghāḥ</i>, “and all the hosts of siddhas bow”.",
 "This verse begins Arjuna’s great hymn, 11.36–44. The first verse, <i>sthāne hṛṣīkeśa</i>, is recited as a protective mantra in some traditions, especially in South India. Commentators note the contrast: the world rejoices, the demons flee, the perfected bow. The same praise brings joy to some and fear to others. Rāmānuja says the Lord’s praise is the world’s delight.",
 "Praise of the good lifts the heart and drives fear away. Try singing or reciting something uplifting today.")
DEEP[37]=("<i>Kasmāc ca te na nameran mahātman</i>, “and why should they not bow to you, great soul”. <i>Garīyase brahmaṇo’py ādi-kartre</i>, “greater even than Brahmā, the first creator”. <i>Ananta deveśa jagan-nivāsa</i>, “Infinite, Lord of gods, home of the world”. <i>Tvam akṣaraṃ sad-asat tat-paraṃ yat</i>, “you are the Imperishable, being, non-being, and beyond”.",
 "<i>Sad asat tat-param</i>: commentators read <i>sat</i> as the manifest effect and <i>asat</i> as the unmanifest cause, and the Lord as beyond both. The Ṛg Veda’s Nāsadīya Sūkta (10.129.1) begins: “there was neither non-being nor being then”. Arjuna’s praise reaches back to the oldest questions.",
 "Bowing to what is greater is not weakness. It is simply seeing things as they are.")
DEEP[38]=("<i>Tvam ādi-devaḥ puruṣaḥ purāṇas</i>, “you are the first God, the ancient Person”. <i>Tvam asya viśvasya paraṃ nidhānam</i>, “the supreme resting place of this universe”. <i>Vettāsi vedyaṃ ca paraṃ ca dhāma</i>, “the knower, the known, the supreme abode”. <i>Tvayā tataṃ viśvam ananta-rūpa</i>, “by you the universe is pervaded, endless form”.",
 "<i>Vettā vedyam</i>, the knower and the known. Śaṅkara reads this as the Lord being both subject and object, ultimately one. Rāmānuja reads it as the Lord knowing all and being the one to be known by all. <i>Nidhāna</i>, resting place or treasure, repeats 9.18 and 11.18. The repetition of titles is typical of hymns: like a garland, each flower adds to the whole.",
 "The one who knows and what is known meet in the sacred. Let your learning lead to reverence.")
DEEP[39]=("<i>Vāyur yamo’gnir varuṇaḥ śaśāṅkaḥ</i>, “wind, Yama, fire, Varuṇa, the moon”. <i>Prajāpatis tvaṃ prapitāmahaś ca</i>, “you are Prajāpati and the great-grandsire”. <i>Namo namas te’stu sahasra-kṛtvaḥ</i>, “salutations, salutations to you a thousand times”. <i>Punaś ca bhūyo’pi namo namas te</i>, “and again, salutations, salutations”.",
 "The Vedic gods named here, wind, death, fire, water and moon, are all seen as forms of one Lord. The repetition of <i>namaḥ</i> recalls the Śrī Rudram of the Yajur Veda, where <i>namo namaḥ</i> repeats through hundreds of lines. Commentators say love that overflows naturally repeats itself; it is not mechanical. Many devotees bow repeatedly in temples for this reason.",
 "When gratitude overflows, let it repeat. Some words are worth saying again and again.")
DEEP[40]=("<i>Namaḥ purastād atha pṛṣṭhatas te</i>, “salutations in front and behind”. <i>Namo’stu te sarvata eva sarva</i>, “salutations on every side, O All”. <i>Ananta-vīryāmita-vikramas tvaṃ</i>, “infinite in power, immeasurable in strength”. <i>Sarvaṃ samāpnoṣi tato’si sarvaḥ</i>, “you pervade all, so you are all”.",
 "Arjuna bows in every direction because there is nowhere the Lord is not. Commentators note the conclusion: <i>sarvaṃ samāpnoṣi tato’si sarvaḥ</i>, “you pervade all, therefore you are all”. Śaṅkara reads it as identity. Rāmānuja reads it as “all is your body”. Madhva reads it as “you fill all”. The devotion is the same in every reading.",
 "Wherever you turn today, you can find something to bow to inwardly. Try it.")
DEEP[41]=("<i>Sakheti matvā prasabhaṃ yad uktaṃ</i>, “thinking of you as a friend, whatever I said rashly”. <i>He kṛṣṇa he yādava he sakheti</i>, “‘O Kṛṣṇa, O Yādava, O friend’”. <i>Ajānatā mahimānaṃ tavedaṃ</i>, “not knowing this greatness of yours”. <i>Mayā pramādāt praṇayena vāpi</i>, “through carelessness or affection”.",
 "Arjuna and Kṛṣṇa had a long friendship. They burned the Khāṇḍava forest together, Kṛṣṇa helped Arjuna win Subhadrā (Kṛṣṇa’s sister) as his wife, and they shared jokes and meals. Bhakti traditions name five moods of devotion: peace (<i>śānta</i>), service (<i>dāsya</i>), friendship (<i>sakhya</i>), parental love (<i>vātsalya</i>) and romantic love (<i>mādhurya</i>). Arjuna is the classic example of <i>sakhya</i>. Commentators say the Lord loves that friendship, and Arjuna’s apology only deepens it.",
 "Look at someone close to you with fresh eyes today. We often take our dearest people for granted.")
DEEP[42]=("<i>Yac cāvahāsārtham asat-kṛto’si</i>, “and whatever disrespect, in jest”. <i>Vihāra-śayyāsana-bhojaneṣu</i>, “while playing, resting, sitting, eating”. <i>Eko’tha vāpy acyuta tat-samakṣaṃ</i>, “alone or in company, Acyuta”. <i>Tat kṣāmaye tvām aham aprameyam</i>, “for that I ask forgiveness of you, the Immeasurable”.",
 "Commentators find this verse touching for its ordinary details: games, rest, meals, jokes in front of others. These are the moments of real friendship. <i>Acyuta</i>, the unfallen, is gently ironic: Arjuna has “fallen” in respect, but Kṛṣṇa has not fallen in love. Śaṅkara says the apology removes any fault. Rāmānuja says the Lord never counted it a fault.",
 "Apologise to someone you have taken lightly. It heals both of you.")
DEEP[43]=("<i>Pitāsi lokasya carācarasya</i>, “you are the father of the moving and unmoving world”. <i>Tvam asya pūjyaś ca gurur garīyān</i>, “its worshipful and greatest teacher”. <i>Na tvat-samo’sty abhyadhikaḥ kuto’nyo</i>, “none equals you; how could another surpass you”. <i>Loka-traye’py apratima-prabhāva</i>, “in the three worlds, one of matchless power”.",
 "Two relationships: father, who gives life, and guru, who gives wisdom. Commentators note <i>garīyān guruḥ</i>, “weightiest teacher”: Kṛṣṇa is the teacher of teachers. Arjuna asked to be taught in 2.7; now he knows who has been teaching him. <i>Apratima</i>, matchless, also means “without image”: no image can contain him, though devotees worship him in images.",
 "Honour those who gave you life and wisdom. In them you glimpse the source of both.")
DEEP[44]=("<i>Tasmāt praṇamya praṇidhāya kāyaṃ</i>, “so, bowing, laying down my body”. <i>Prasādaye tvām aham īśam īḍyam</i>, “I seek your grace, adorable Lord”. <i>Piteva putrasya sakheva sakhyuḥ</i>, “as a father with a son, a friend with a friend”. <i>Priyaḥ priyāyārhasi deva soḍhum</i>, “a lover with the beloved, please bear with me”.",
 "<i>Praṇidhāya kāyam</i>, “laying the body down”, is full prostration, <i>sāṣṭāṅga praṇāma</i>, with eight limbs touching the ground. The three images later shape bhakti theology. Commentators note that each relationship forgives easily: a father forgives a son’s mischief, a friend a friend’s jokes, a lover a beloved’s faults. Rāmānuja says the Lord’s love is greater than all three combined.",
 "Love forgives. Ask for forgiveness where you need it, and give it freely.")
DEEP[45]=("<i>Adṛṣṭa-pūrvaṃ hṛṣito’smi dṛṣṭvā</i>, “I am thrilled to have seen what was never seen”. <i>Bhayena ca pravyathitaṃ mano me</i>, “and my mind is shaken with fear”. <i>Tad eva me darśaya deva rūpaṃ</i>, “show me that form, O God”. <i>Prasīda deveśa jagan-nivāsa</i>, “be gracious, Lord of gods, home of the world”.",
 "Arjuna’s honesty is complete: joy and fear together. Commentators say the cosmic form is too much for human love to rest in; the heart longs for a form it can approach. Śrī Vaiṣṇavas speak of the Lord’s five forms: the supreme form, the cosmic emanations, the avatāras, the inner controller and the temple image. Each answers a different need. <i>Prasīda</i>, “be gracious”, repeats a third time.",
 "The infinite inspires awe; the personal inspires love. Both have their place in a life of faith.")
DEEP[46]=("<i>Kirīṭinaṃ gadinaṃ cakra-hastam</i>, “crowned, with mace, discus in hand”. <i>Icchāmi tvāṃ draṣṭum ahaṃ tathaiva</i>, “I wish to see you just as before”. <i>Tenaiva rūpeṇa catur-bhujena</i>, “in that four-armed form”. <i>Sahasra-bāho bhava viśva-mūrte</i>, “become so, thousand-armed one, of universal form”.",
 "The four-armed Viṣṇu holds conch, discus, mace and lotus. In the Bhāgavata (10.3), Kṛṣṇa first appeared to Vasudeva and Devakī in this four-armed form at his birth, then became a human child at their request. Commentators draw a parallel: Devakī, like Arjuna, asked for the gentler form. Madhva says Kṛṣṇa’s true form is always divine, whether two-armed or four.",
 "It is natural to need a form of the Divine we can relate to. Let your devotion find its form.")
DEEP[47]=("<i>Mayā prasannena tavārjunedaṃ</i>, “by me, pleased with you, Arjuna”. <i>Rūpaṃ paraṃ darśitam ātma-yogāt</i>, “this supreme form has been shown, by my own power”. <i>Tejo-mayaṃ viśvam anantam ādyaṃ</i>, “radiant, universal, infinite, primal”. <i>Yan me tvad-anyena na dṛṣṭa-pūrvam</i>, “which none but you has seen before”.",
 "<i>Prasannena</i>, “pleased”, is the answer to Arjuna’s repeated <i>prasīda</i>. Commentators note that other cosmic visions in the epic were partial or different. Yaśodā saw the universe in the child’s mouth, and the Kaurava court saw a cosmic form. This one, with the future of the war inside it, was for Arjuna alone. Śaṅkara stresses the grace; Rāmānuja stresses the Lord’s love for Arjuna.",
 "The greatest gifts come by grace. Receive them with gratitude, not pride.")
DEEP[48]=("<i>Na veda-yajñādhyayanair na dānair</i>, “not by Vedas, sacrifices, study, nor by gifts”. <i>Na ca kriyābhir na tapobhir ugraiḥ</i>, “nor by rituals, nor by fierce austerities”. <i>Evaṃ-rūpaḥ śakya ahaṃ nṛ-loke</i>, “can I be seen in this form in the human world”. <i>Draṣṭuṃ tvad-anyena kuru-pravīra</i>, “by any other than you, hero of the Kurus”.",
 "The list covers every religious effort of the time. Commentators do not read it as rejecting these practices; the Gītā elsewhere praises sacrifice, charity and austerity (18.5). They purify, but they do not compel the vision. The Kaṭha Upaniṣad (1.2.23) says the Self is not gained by teaching, intellect or much learning; it is gained by the one it chooses.",
 "Effort prepares the ground. What finally opens the door is a gift.")
DEEP[49]=("<i>Mā te vyathā mā ca vimūḍha-bhāvo</i>, “do not be afraid or bewildered”. <i>Dṛṣṭvā rūpaṃ ghoram īdṛṅ mamedam</i>, “at seeing this terrible form of mine”. <i>Vyapeta-bhīḥ prīta-manāḥ punas tvaṃ</i>, “free from fear, glad at heart, again”. <i>Tad eva me rūpam idaṃ prapaśya</i>, “behold that form of mine”.",
 "<i>Mā te vyathā</i>, “let there be no fear for you”. Kṛṣṇa’s first word after the vision is comfort, like a parent after a storm. Commentators note the balance of majesty, <i>aiśvarya</i>, and sweetness, <i>mādhurya</i>, in the Lord. Rāmānuja’s tradition lists <i>vātsalya</i>, motherly tenderness, as one of the Lord’s chief qualities. The verse completes the arc from <i>paśya</i> (11.5) to <i>prapaśya</i>, behold again, now without fear.",
 "After a great shock, gentle words heal. Offer them freely to someone shaken today.")
DEEP[50]=("<i>Ity arjunaṃ vāsudevas tathoktvā</i>, “having spoken thus to Arjuna, Vāsudeva”. <i>Svakaṃ rūpaṃ darśayām āsa bhūyaḥ</i>, “showed his own form again”. <i>Āśvāsayām āsa ca bhītam enaṃ</i>, “and comforted the frightened one”. <i>Bhūtvā punaḥ saumya-vapur mahātmā</i>, “the great soul, taking gentle form again”.",
 "This is the last triṣṭubh verse in the chapter; the gentle form returns in the gentle metre of the next verse. Commentators admire the craft: even the poetry calms down. <i>Āśvāsayām āsa</i>, “he consoled”, from <i>śvas</i>, to breathe: Kṛṣṇa lets Arjuna breathe again. Sañjaya, telling the blind king, quietly includes the tenderness along with the terror.",
 "After facing something overwhelming, let yourself breathe. Return slowly to the familiar.")
DEEP[51]=("<i>Dṛṣṭvedaṃ mānuṣaṃ rūpaṃ</i>, “seeing this human form”. <i>Tava saumyaṃ janārdana</i>, “your gentle form, Janārdana”. <i>Idānīm asmi saṃvṛttaḥ</i>, “now I am composed”. <i>Sa-cetāḥ prakṛtiṃ gataḥ</i>, “restored to my normal self”.",
 "Commentators note that Arjuna is restored, but not unchanged. Teachers of meditation often say the same: after deep experiences, one must return to ordinary life, and the test of an experience is how one lives afterward. Arjuna will now fight with understanding. Some traditions compare this to waking from a dream: the dream is over, but its lesson stays.",
 "After a big experience, return to ordinary life changed but steady. That is where it bears fruit.")
DEEP[52]=("<i>Su-durdarśam idaṃ rūpaṃ</i>, “very hard to see is this form”. <i>Dṛṣṭavān asi yan mama</i>, “which you have seen of mine”. <i>Devā apy asya rūpasya</i>, “even the gods, of this form”. <i>Nityaṃ darśana-kāṅkṣiṇaḥ</i>, “are always longing for a sight”.",
 "<i>Darśana</i>, seeing the Divine, is central to Hindu worship: devotees go to temples for darśan, to see and be seen by the deity. Commentators debate which form is meant: the cosmic form, or the four-armed form, or the human Kṛṣṇa now present. Many devotional teachers say the human form seen with love is the rarest: the gods longed to see Kṛṣṇa as a cowherd boy.",
 "What is in front of you may be more precious than you know. Look again, with love.")
DEEP[53]=("<i>Nāhaṃ vedair na tapasā</i>, “not by the Vedas, nor by austerity”. <i>Na dānena na cejyayā</i>, “nor by gifts, nor by sacrifice”. <i>Śakya evaṃ-vidho draṣṭuṃ</i>, “can I be seen in such a form”. <i>Dṛṣṭavān asi māṃ yathā</i>, “as you have seen me”.",
 "This verse nearly repeats 11.48. Commentators say repetition in scripture marks importance. Śaṅkara says it prepares the key verse that follows. Rāmānuja notes that practices are not useless; they simply cannot command grace. The four practices here, study, austerity, charity and worship, are the pillars of Vedic religious life.",
 "When a teacher repeats something, pay attention. It usually matters most.")
DEEP[54]=("<i>Bhaktyā tv ananyayā śakya</i>, “but by undivided devotion, possible”. <i>Aham evaṃ-vidho’rjuna</i>, “am I in this form, Arjuna”. <i>Jñātuṃ draṣṭuṃ ca tattvena</i>, “to be known and seen in truth”. <i>Praveṣṭuṃ ca parantapa</i>, “and to be entered, scorcher of foes”.",
 "Three stages: <i>jñātum</i>, to know; <i>draṣṭum</i>, to see; <i>praveṣṭum</i>, to enter. Commentators say knowing is understanding, seeing is direct experience, entering is union. Śaṅkara reads entering as oneness with Brahman. Rāmānuja reads it as reaching the Lord and enjoying him. Madhva reads it as entering the Lord’s presence. The key word is <i>ananya bhakti</i>, devotion to nothing else, which returns from 8.22 and 9.22. This prepares chapter 12, which is entirely about devotion.",
 "Love opens doors that effort alone cannot. Let your practice be full of love.")
DEEP[55]=("<i>Mat-karma-kṛn mat-paramo</i>, “who works for me, has me as supreme goal”. <i>Mad-bhaktaḥ saṅga-varjitaḥ</i>, “devoted to me, free from attachment”. <i>Nirvairaḥ sarva-bhūteṣu</i>, “without enmity toward any being”. <i>Yaḥ sa mām eti pāṇḍava</i>, “he comes to me, Pāṇḍava”.",
 "Śaṅkara called this verse the essence of the Gītā’s teaching, <i>sarva-gītā-śāstra-sāra</i>. Commentators note how it gathers karma yoga (work for the Lord), bhakti (devotion), vairāgya (non-attachment) and universal goodwill. <i>Nirvaira</i>, without enmity, is remarkable on a battlefield: Arjuna must fight, but without hatred. Gandhi took this verse as a guide for non-violent resistance. The colophon names chapter 11 Viśvarūpa-Darśana Yoga.",
 "Work for the highest, love, let go, and hate no one. Everything else follows.")
